WARDEN Badge Office — JWT

> **Challenge:** WARDEN Badge Office — JWT  
> **Category:** Web / Authentication / JWT  
> **Difficulty:** Intermediate

## Objective

The objective of this challenge was to gain WARDEN-level access by manipulating the authentication token used by the Badge Office.

---

## Investigation

The Badge Office used a JSON Web Token (JWT) for authentication.
When inspecting the token, I noticed that it used the `RS256` signing algorithm and referenced a public key through a `kid` value.
Normally, `RS256` uses an asymmetric key pair:

- The private key is used to sign the JWT.
- The public key is used to verify the JWT.

This meant that simply modifying the token would normally cause the signature to become invalid.

### Identifying the Vulnerability

The important detail was that the application appeared to trust the algorithm specified in the JWT itself.
This suggested a potential JWT algorithm-confusion attack.

---

## Exploitation

### Step 1 — Change the Algorithm

The original JWT used:
RS256

### Step 2 - Use the Public Key as the Secret 

The application already had access to the public PEM associated with the original RS256 configuration.
Because the server accepted the attacker-controlled alg value, I could use this public key as the HMAC secret when generating an HS256 signature.

### Step 3 - Modify the Role

I modified the relevant JWT claims so that the token represented the required WARDEN role while keeping the expected kid.
The resulting token was submitted to the Badge Office and was accepted.


## Solution 
The challenge was solved by exploiting an RS256-to-HS256 algorithm-confusion vulnerability.
The application failed to enforce the expected signing algorithm independently of the attacker-controlled JWT header.

## Key Takeaways 
- JWT algorithms should be explicitly enforced by the server.
- RS256 and HS256 use fundamentally different key models.
- Algorithm confusion can allow a public verification key to be abused as an HMAC secret.
- Authorization claims should only be trusted after the token has been securely validated.
